## Write y = f(x)
y &= 3x - 2
## Swap x and y
x &= 3y - 2
## Solve for y
x + 2 &= 3y
y &= \frac{x+2}{3}
## Write the inverse
f^{-1}(x) &= \frac{x+2}{3}
ANSWER: f^{-1}(x) = \frac{x+2}{3}

## Differentiate the volume of a sphere
V &= \frac{4}{3}\pi r^3
\frac{dV}{dr} &= 4\pi r^2
## Use the chain rule for the rate of change of the radius
The rate given is $\frac{dV}{dt} = 250$, so we need $\frac{dr}{dt} = \frac{dr}{dV}\times\frac{dV}{dt}$.
\frac{dr}{dt} &= \frac{1}{4\pi r^2}\times 250
## Substitute r = 20
\frac{dr}{dt} &= \frac{250}{4\pi (20)^2}
&= \frac{250}{1600\pi}
&= \frac{5}{32\pi}
ANSWER: \frac{5}{32\pi} \text{ cm/s}

## Differentiate the surface area of a sphere
A &= 4\pi r^2
\frac{dA}{dr} &= 8\pi r
## Use the chain rule with the result from part (i)
$\frac{dA}{dt} = \frac{dA}{dr}\times\frac{dr}{dt}$, and $\frac{dr}{dt} = \frac{5}{32\pi}$ when $r = 20$.
\frac{dA}{dt} &= 8\pi(20)\times\frac{5}{32\pi}
&= \frac{800\pi}{32\pi}
&= 25
ANSWER: 25 \text{ cm}^2/\text{s}

## Write the average value as an integral
The ball is in the air from $x = 0$ to $x = 10$. Average height $= \frac{1}{b-a}\int_a^b f(x)\,dx$.
\text{average} &= \frac{1}{10-0}\int_0^{10}(-x^2+10x)\,dx
## Integrate
&= \frac{1}{10}\left[-\frac{x^3}{3} + 5x^2\right]_0^{10}
## Substitute the limits
&= \frac{1}{10}\left[\left(-\frac{1000}{3} + 500\right) - 0\right]
&= \frac{1}{10}\times\frac{500}{3}
&= \frac{50}{3}
ANSWER: \frac{50}{3} \text{ m}

## Spot the pattern in the distances
The distances are $4, 2, 1, \frac12, \frac14, \dots$, each half the one before, so this is a geometric sequence.
a &= 4
r &= \frac{2}{4}
r &= \frac{1}{2}
## Use the sum of n terms and set it equal to 7.9375
S_n &= \frac{a(1-r^n)}{1-r}
S_n &= \frac{4\left(1-\left(\frac12\right)^n\right)}{1-\frac12}
S_n &= 8\left(1-\frac{1}{2^n}\right)
8\left(1-\frac{1}{2^n}\right) &= 7.9375
## Solve for n
1-\frac{1}{2^n} &= 0.9921875
\frac{1}{2^n} &= 0.0078125
\frac{1}{2^n} &= \frac{1}{128}
2^n &= 128
n &= 7
ANSWER: 7 \text{ stages}

## Use the sum to infinity
The ratio $r = \frac12$ is between $-1$ and $1$, so the sum to infinity exists.
S_\infty &= \frac{a}{1-r}
S_\infty &= \frac{4}{1-\frac{1}{2}}
&= \frac{4}{\frac12}
&= 8
ANSWER: 8 \text{ units}

## Complete the table
Each stage changes only one co-ordinate. Stages 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 change $x$; stages 2, 4, 6, 8 change $y$. The distance halves each stage, and the direction goes right, up, left, down.
Change in $x$: $+4,\ 0,\ -1,\ 0,\ +\frac14,\ 0,\ -\frac{1}{16},\ 0,\ +\frac{1}{64}$.
Change in $y$: $0,\ +2,\ 0,\ -\frac12,\ 0,\ +\frac18,\ 0,\ -\frac{1}{32},\ 0$.
## Spot the two geometric series
The non-zero $x$ changes are $4, -1, \frac14, -\frac1{16}, \dots$. First term $4$, each term is $-\frac14$ of the last.
r_x &= -1 \div 4
The non-zero $y$ changes are $2, -\frac12, \frac18, -\frac1{32}, \dots$. First term $2$, common ratio $-\frac14$ again.
## Final x co-ordinate: sum to infinity of the x changes
S_\infty &= \frac{4}{1-\left(-\frac14\right)}
&= \frac{4}{\frac54}
&= \frac{16}{5}
## Final y co-ordinate: sum to infinity of the y changes
S_\infty &= \frac{2}{1-\left(-\frac14\right)}
&= \frac{2}{\frac54}
&= \frac{8}{5}
ANSWER: \left(\frac{16}{5},\ \frac{8}{5}\right) = (3.2,\ 1.6)

## Write the sum-to-product formulas
\cos P + \cos Q &= 2\cos\frac{P + Q}{2}\cos\frac{P - Q}{2}
\sin P - \sin Q &= 2\cos\frac{P + Q}{2}\sin\frac{P - Q}{2}
## Apply them with P = 7A and Q = A
\frac{\cos 7A + \cos A}{\sin 7A - \sin A} &= \frac{2\cos\frac{7A + A}{2}\cos\frac{7A - A}{2}}{2\cos\frac{7A + A}{2}\sin\frac{7A - A}{2}}
&= \frac{2\cos 4A\cos 3A}{2\cos 4A\sin 3A}
## Cancel the common factor
Divide top and bottom by $2\cos 4A$.
&= \frac{\cos 3A}{\sin 3A}
&= \cot 3A
ANSWER: \frac{\cos 7A + \cos A}{\sin 7A - \sin A} = \cot 3A

## Use the half-angle formula for cos squared
From $\cos 2\theta = 2\cos^2\theta - 1$ we get $\cos^2\theta = \frac{1}{2}(1 + \cos 2\theta)$.
\cos^2\theta &= \frac{1}{2}(1 + \cos 2\theta)
## Substitute cos 2θ = 1/9
\cos^2\theta &= \frac{1}{2}\left(1 + \frac{1}{9}\right)
&= \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{10}{9}\right)
&= \frac{5}{9}
## Take the square root
Take both the positive and negative roots.
\cos\theta &= \pm\sqrt{\frac{5}{9}}
\cos\theta &= \pm\frac{\sqrt{5}}{3}
ANSWER: \cos\theta = \pm\frac{\sqrt{5}}{3}

## Match corresponding sides of the similar triangles
$A$ matches $D$, $B$ matches $B$, $D$ matches $C$, so $[AB]$ matches $[DB]$ and $[BD]$ matches $[BC]$.
\frac{|AB|}{|DB|} &= \frac{|BD|}{|BC|}
## Substitute the given lengths
\frac{x}{y} &= \frac{y}{1}
y^2 &= x
## Take the square root
$y$ is a length, so it is positive.
y &= \sqrt{x}
ANSWER: y = \sqrt{x}

## Write the probability of a hit and a miss
P(\text{John hits}) &= \frac{1}{5},\ P(\text{John misses}) = \frac{4}{5}
P(\text{David hits}) &= \frac{1}{6},\ P(\text{David misses}) = \frac{5}{6}
P(\text{Mike hits}) &= \frac{1}{4},\ P(\text{Mike misses}) = \frac{3}{4}
## Way 1: John and David hit, Mike misses
P(\text{Way 1}) &= \frac{1}{5}\times\frac{1}{6}\times\frac{3}{4}
&= \frac{3}{120}
## Way 2: John and Mike hit, David misses
P(\text{Way 2}) &= \frac{1}{5}\times\frac{5}{6}\times\frac{1}{4}
&= \frac{5}{120}
## Way 3: David and Mike hit, John misses
P(\text{Way 3}) &= \frac{4}{5}\times\frac{1}{6}\times\frac{1}{4}
&= \frac{4}{120}
## Way 4: all three hit
P(\text{Way 4}) &= \frac{1}{5}\times\frac{1}{6}\times\frac{1}{4}
&= \frac{1}{120}
## Add the four ways
The ways cannot happen together, so add their probabilities.
P(\text{win}) &= \frac{3}{120}+\frac{5}{120}+\frac{4}{120}+\frac{1}{120}
&= \frac{13}{120}
ANSWER: \frac{13}{120}
